# Replay contract: a replicated journal (safety)

Version 3, 2026-10-21 (versions 1 and 2 the same day). Clause ids are never reused: J1.2 and J1.4 are retired, and a tag naming either is stale. Both arms build to this contract; each arm's specification (`spec-D.md` or `spec-F.md`) says how.

## Terms
Member, journal, entry, epoch, coordinator, configuration and committed are as defined in the arm's specification. An entry is **committed** when a coordinator marks it committed (its commit index reaches the entry's index); a member records the moment it learns of it.

## Clauses
- **J1.1 (one coordinator per epoch).** At most one member is coordinator in any given epoch, over the whole run.
- **J1.5 (committed entries persist).** If the entry at index i is committed in epoch e, then (a) every member that is elected coordinator in an epoch higher than e has that entry at index i when it is elected, and (b) no member removes an entry at an index at or below its own commit index.
- **J1.3 (one change at a time).** Every configuration entry differs from the configuration entry that precedes it in the same journal (or from the initial configuration, for the first) by exactly one member added or removed.

J1.1 and J1.5 are safety properties of the system. J1.3 is a property of each journal and holds by construction if the specification is followed. There is no liveness clause in this contract.

Retired: **J1.2** (version 1), "if an entry is committed at index i, then from then on every member's journal that has an entry at index i has that same entry there, and every member that becomes coordinator afterwards has that entry at index i". Its first half is violated by a member that still holds a stale, uncommitted entry at an index committed elsewhere, which the design allows. Retired: **J1.4** (version 2), whose part (b) read "no member that holds that entry at index i ever removes it from index i"; a member that does not know the entry is committed can have its copy overwritten by a stale coordinator of an older epoch, which a correct design allows. J1.5 states what was meant.

## Observable events
Each recorded by the member it concerns, with that member's own sequence number and `causes` links, in the format of `replay/observe.md`:
`Elected(member, epoch, config)`, `Appended(member, index, epoch, kind, payload)` (kind is `command` or `config`), `Truncated(member, from_index)`, `Committed(member, index, epoch)`, `ConfigInEffect(member, config, index)`, `ChangeRequested(member, op, target)`, `ChangeReplied(member, op, target, status)`.
# Replay failure model

Version 1, fixed 2026-10-21 for the known-bug replay. Cell ids are never reused.

The system has five member names, `m1` to `m5`. A run starts with an initial configuration given by the scenario (for example `{m1, m2, m3, m4}`); members not in it are started with the initial configuration too, and an empty journal, so that they can be added. Configuration change requests come from an administrator, at any member, at any time.

## Cells
- **G1.1 Crash and restart.** Any member may stop and later restart with its durable state (`epoch`, `votedFor`, `journal`) and nothing else.
- **G1.2 Message loss, delay, reordering and duplication.** Any message may be lost, delayed for any finite time, delivered out of order, or delivered more than once. Messages are never corrupted.
- **G1.3 Partition.** Links between members go down and come back, in either or both directions; while a link is down, messages sent over it are lost. (This is G1.2 applied systematically; it is a separate cell so that scenarios can name it.)
- **G1.4 Administrative change.** An add or remove request may arrive at any member at any moment. A request that the receiving member rejects or that times out may be retried at another member.

Timeouts are the only clocks; no member's clock is compared with another's.

## Not in this model
Byzantine behaviour, storage loss or corruption, clock skew beyond arbitrary timeouts, non-voting members, snapshots.
# Specification F: a replicated journal with single-member configuration changes

This is the design you are to build, exactly as written. It is not a contract you may satisfy by any design you like: where this specification says how something is done, do it that way, even if you believe another way is better. If you find a place where you think the design is wrong, do not fix it; record it in your README as an interpretation or a concern, and build what is written. Where the specification is silent, choose, and record the choice.

## Vocabulary
- **Member.** A process that stores a journal and takes part in agreement. Members are named `m1`, `m2`, … .
- **Journal.** A sequence of entries, indexed from 1. Each entry carries the **epoch** in which it was first appended and a payload. A payload is either a **command** (opaque) or a **configuration** (a set of member names).
- **Epoch.** A non-negative integer each member stores, 0 when the member first starts. Epochs only increase.
- **Coordinator.** A member that has won an election for its current epoch. Other members are **followers** or **candidates**.
- **Configuration of a member.** The payload of the latest configuration entry in that member's journal, whether or not that entry is committed. If the journal has no configuration entry, the member's configuration is the initial configuration it was started with.
- **Majority of a configuration C.** Any set containing more than half of the members of C.
- **Committed.** An entry is committed once the coordinator that appended it, or a later coordinator, has applied the commitment rule below to it or to a later entry.

## State each member keeps
Durable (survives a crash): `epoch`, `votedFor` (a member name or none, for the current epoch), `journal`. Durable state is written to stable storage before the member responds to any request.
Volatile (lost on a crash, reset on restart): `role` (follower on restart), `commitIndex` (0 on restart), and, for a coordinator, `nextIndex` and `matchIndex` for each other member.

## Messages
Every message carries the sender's `epoch`. On receiving any message with an epoch higher than its own, a member sets its `epoch` to that value, clears `votedFor`, and becomes a follower, before processing the message further. A member ignores a request whose epoch is lower than its own, replying with its own epoch. Requests and replies may be lost, delayed or reordered. A member that gets no timely reply to a request retries it; requests to different members are sent in parallel.

Members process incoming requests without consulting their own configuration. In particular a member grants votes to, and accepts journal extensions from, members that are not in its configuration.

### Election
- Each member's election timeout is chosen at random from a fixed range. A follower's timer is reset when it receives an extend request from the current coordinator or grants a ballot. A follower or candidate whose timer expires becomes a **candidate**: it increments `epoch`, sets `votedFor` to itself, resets its election timer, and sends a **ballot request** to every other member of its own configuration, carrying the index and epoch of its last journal entry.
- A member grants a ballot if (a) the request's epoch is at least its own, (b) it has not voted for a different member in that epoch, and (c) the candidate's journal is at least as up to date as its own: the candidate's last entry has a higher epoch, or the same epoch and an index at least as large. When it grants, it records `votedFor` durably before replying.
- A candidate that receives grants from a majority of its own configuration (counting itself if it is a member of it) becomes coordinator of that epoch. A candidate that hears from a coordinator of an epoch at least its own becomes a follower.

### Journal extension
- A coordinator sends each other member of its configuration an **extend request**: the index and epoch of the entry immediately before the entries it sends (`prevIndex`, `prevEpoch`), zero or more entries, and the coordinator's `commitIndex`. The entries sent to a member start at that member's `nextIndex`, so `prevIndex` is `nextIndex` − 1; with no entries the request serves as a heartbeat. The coordinator retries extend requests to a member indefinitely until the member stores every entry.
- A receiver whose journal has no entry at `prevIndex` with epoch `prevEpoch` replies failure. Otherwise it deletes any existing entry that conflicts with a sent entry (same index, different epoch) together with all entries after it, appends the sent entries it does not already have, and, if the coordinator's `commitIndex` is greater than its own, sets its `commitIndex` to the smaller of the coordinator's `commitIndex` and the index of the last entry the request carried (`prevIndex` plus the number of entries sent); `commitIndex` never decreases. It replies success. A `prevIndex` of 0 always matches. If entries it deleted included a configuration entry, its configuration falls back to the latest configuration entry that remains.
- On becoming coordinator, a member sets `nextIndex` for every other member to its last journal index plus one and `matchIndex` to 0, and sends heartbeats at a regular interval shorter than the election timeout. On failure it decrements `nextIndex` for that member and retries; on success it sets that member's `matchIndex` to the index of the last entry the request carried, and `nextIndex` to one more than that.

### Commitment rule
Whenever there is an index N greater than its `commitIndex` such that (a) the entry at N carries the coordinator's current epoch, and (b) a majority of the coordinator's configuration (counting the coordinator if it is a member) have `matchIndex ≥ N`, the coordinator sets `commitIndex` to N, marking the entry at N committed. Committing N commits every earlier entry. Entries from earlier epochs are therefore committed only indirectly, when a later entry of the current epoch is committed.

## Configuration changes
Configurations are entries in the journal and are replicated like any other entry.
- A configuration takes effect on a member as soon as the entry is in its journal, before it is committed. Every member always uses the latest configuration in its journal, for elections and for commitment.
- The commitment of a configuration entry is decided by a majority of the new configuration it contains.
- A change adds exactly one member to, or removes exactly one member from, the configuration in which it is made; larger changes are made as a series of such changes. The design restricts changes to one member at a time because any majority of the old configuration then overlaps any majority of the new one, so the cluster cannot split into two independent majorities.

### Adding a member (handled by the member that receives the request)
1. If not the coordinator, reply "not coordinator".
2. Catch the new member up for a fixed number of rounds of extend requests. Reply "timeout" if the new member does not make progress for an election timeout, or if the last round takes longer than an election timeout. If the new member's journal already matches the coordinator's, catch-up is complete at once.
3. Wait until the previous configuration entry in the coordinator's journal is committed. (If the journal has no configuration entry, the initial configuration counts as committed.)
4. Append a configuration entry containing the current configuration plus the new member; commit it using a majority of the new configuration.
5. Reply "ok".

### Removing a member
1. If not the coordinator, reply "not coordinator".
2. Wait until the previous configuration entry in the coordinator's journal is committed. (If the journal has no configuration entry, the initial configuration counts as committed.)
3. Append a configuration entry containing the current configuration without the removed member; commit it using a majority of the new configuration.
4. Reply "ok". If the coordinator removed itself, it steps down after the entry is committed.

A coordinator does not begin a new change while the previous configuration entry in its journal is uncommitted.

## What the design does not include
Client requests other than commands and configuration changes; reads; snapshots or journal compaction; non-voting members. A command is an opaque payload appended by the coordinator on request.
# Replay scenario format (shared interface)

Version 1, fixed 2026-10-21. Both sides compile a scenario: the model side into a TLC configuration that constrains the environment to the scenario's steps while exploring every interleaving it leaves open; the implementation side into fault injection and requests against a running system, sampled over seeds.

## Shape
```yaml
scenario: <name>
derives: [J1.5, G1.3]           # clauses and cells exercised
initial: [m1, m2, m3, m4]       # initial configuration; m5 exists too and is started with it
steps:
  - at: <trigger>
    do: <action>
end: {settle: 100}               # events after the last step's action
bound: {events: 400}
```

## Triggers
`start`, or an observable event from `replay/contract.md` with optional field filters, for example `{event: Elected, member: m2}`, `{event: Committed, member: m2, index: 3}`, `{event: Appended, member: m5, kind: config}`. Step 1 is armed at the start; step *i* is armed when step *i*−1's action is taken, and matches only events emitted after it was armed (the action's own synchronous events count). Once a trigger fires, the action is taken at some later point: the model explores every such point, the implementation samples one.

## Actions
```yaml
do: {add: m5, at: m1}                 # an administrator asks m1 to add m5 (G1.4)
do: {remove: m4, at: m2}              # asks m2 to remove m4 (G1.4)
do: {crash: m3}                       # G1.1
do: {restart: m3}                     # G1.1
do: {cut: [[m1, m2], [m1, m3]]}       # each listed link down in both directions (G1.3)
do: {drop: [[m2, m1]]}                # messages from m2 to m1 lost; the reverse untouched (G1.3)
do: {heal: true}                      # every link restored
do: {command: c1, at: m1}             # an administrator asks m1 to append command c1; a member that is not coordinator ignores it
```
Three selectors may stand for a member name anywhere one is allowed, resolved when the step's trigger fires: `coordinator`, the member most recently `Elected`; `follower`, a member that is up and is not `coordinator` (the model explores every such member, the implementation picks the lowest-numbered); `crashed`, the member most recently crashed by a `crash` step (a scenario that uses it before any crash is rejected; likewise `coordinator` before any `Elected`). A self-pair in `cut` or `drop` is ignored; link failures accumulate until `heal`.

## End and bound
`end: {settle: N}`: the run continues for N events after the last step's action is taken, then stops. A violation of J1.1, J1.3 or J1.5 witnessed at any point is `FAIL <clause>`. If `bound` is reached before every step's action is taken, the outcome is `UNEXPLAINED steps` (the scenario never reached its point), never PASS. Otherwise the outcome is `PASS`. The model side explores every behaviour within its bounds that takes every step and at most N further events, with every prefix of such behaviours; it reports `UNEXPLAINED steps` if no behaviour within its bounds takes every step.

## Where scenarios live
Shared scenarios are in `replay/scenarios/<name>.yaml`. Each side's runner also accepts a path to a scenario file anywhere, because the steward may run scenarios the authors have not seen.
# Replay observability schema (shared interface)

Version 1, fixed 2026-10-21. Both sides write one JSON object per line to `<side>/observe/<run>.jsonl`; the model side from a TLC trace, the implementation side live.

```json
{"proc": "m2", "seq": 14, "event": "Committed", "index": 3, "epoch": 2,
 "causes": [{"proc": "m2", "seq": 12}], "run": "<run id>", "side": "impl"}
```
- `proc`, `seq`: the member the event concerns and its own counter. There is no cross-member clock; wall time, if kept, goes in a separate file for humans.
- Event fields are exactly those in `replay/contract.md`.
- `causes`: the records this one follows from, by `(proc, seq)`. An `Appended` on a follower names the coordinator's `Appended` of the same entry; a `Committed` names the `Appended` of the entry committed; an `Elected` names the ballot grants that elected it, recorded as the voters' `(proc, seq)` of their latest record when they granted; a `ConfigInEffect` names the `Appended` or `Truncated` that caused it.
- Fault steps are recorded as `Fault` records from `proc: "env"` with the step's action as fields and, for `cut` and `drop`, the resolved directed links.

## The question a trace must answer
For any violation of J1.1 or J1.5: which configuration entries each involved member had in effect at each election and each commitment, and which members' grants or acknowledgements those decisions counted. A trace that cannot answer this has failed the observability layer's own test.

