## Concerns after running

<"'!-- derives: J1.1, J1.3, J1.5, G1.1, G1.2, G1.3, G1.4 -->

No unmutated shared run witnessed a contract failure. All predictions matched
for seeds 1, 2 and 3, including all 24 deliberately broken runs. See
[the complete report](report.md) for predictions beside every result and trace
links. All 48 shared results and trace bytes matched on repetition, and matched
again after the documented empty-initial parser clarification. No protocol
change was made in response to a shared result.

S6 exhausted exactly 400 records having taken two of three actions in each seed.
The final action, heal, was waiting for a new Elected event. In seed 2 m1 led the
initial four-voter configuration and the partition split those voters into two
pairs. In seeds 1 and 3 m5 was elected while outside the initial configuration;
it remained connected to all four voters across S6'"'s cut. None of the three
runs emitted a later Elected event. The outcome remains UNEXPLAINED steps,
without a liveness or correctness conclusion.

The outsider election is a direct consequence of the specification's \"a
follower or candidate whose timer expires\" and its rule that receivers process
requests without consulting membership. It also occurs in S1--S5, S7 and S8
for seeds 1 and 3. Do not infer that every named scenario exercised the topology
its author may have had in mind. The unresolved concern about successive
configuration transitions remains; these bounded samples do not discriminate
it. No formal-model artifact was read or compared, and this repository's
mutation results supply only the implementation half of cross-family evidence.

Checker fixtures distinguish forbidden committed truncation and missing
higher-epoch entries from permitted stale-follower truncation and post-restart
commit-index reset. Vet and race-enabled tests passed. An unfamiliar scenario
name passed all three seeds with the same process-event content as S3 after
normalizing only the run id. Explicit empty-initial runs passed all three seeds;
a missing initial field was rejected as preregistered.

## Declared limitations

<"'!-- derives: J1.1, J1.3, J1.5, G1.1, G1.2, G1.3, G1.4 -->

* This is a bounded, cooperative process execution. Crashes occur at atomic
  mailbox boundaries, with fully persisted durable state. Scenarios requiring
  a crash in the middle of a storage operation, independent disk failure, or
  OS-process death are not represented. Disk corruption/loss is outside G1.1;
  the atomic-boundary choice keeps deterministic scheduling reviewable.
* G1.2 samples loss, reordering, duplication and finite delays; the fixed delay
  distribution does not explore arbitrary long finite message delays. The
  scenario format exposes systematic directional/bidirectional loss and healing,
  but has no action for selecting a particular packet or delay. Such adversarial
  histories can be absent from every sampled run. No exhaustive claim is made.
* Auxiliary scheduler, ballot and acknowledgement records consume the bound
  and settling budget along with contract events. A scenario needing many
  protocol operations between contract events may exhaust the budget earlier
  than a model counting only contract events. The choice was preregistered;
  comparisons with different event accounting remain unexplained until checked.
* Action completion means the request/fault was submitted. It does not assert
  that an administrative request eventually succeeded. Requests reaching a
  crashed process disappear; leadership loss abandons in-flight administrator
  work, as recorded in Choices. Scenarios must filter on ChangeReplied when
  success matters. The checker enforces safety only.
* Invocations reusing a scenario name, mutation and seed overwrite that run'"'s
  observe/state files. Concurrent CLI invocations with that same identity are
  unsupported. The steward can use any filename and scenario name, but distinct
  names that sanitize to the same filename also share output paths. This is
  output naming, not a refusal or a protocol decision based on scenario name.
* Only m1--m5, the supplied message/action grammar and version-3 safety clauses
  are implemented. This is the failure model's fixed member universe. There is
  no formal model here, no model mutation, and no inference of correctness from
  agreement. The implementation mutants are intentionally broad; killing them
  demonstrates sensitivity to those faults, not coverage of every possible bug.

## Running

<"'!-- derives: J1.1, J1.3, J1.5, G1.1, G1.2, G1.3, G1.4 -->

From any working directory, with scenario paths relative to that directory:

```sh
/path/to/repository/impl/run.sh /path/to/scenario.yaml --seeds 3
/path/to/repository/impl/run.sh --mutations --seeds 3
/path/to/repository/impl/run.sh /path/to/scenario.yaml --seeds 3 --mutation S8
```

The launcher builds with `/usr/local/go/bin/go`. Runtime imports are only the
standard library and yaml.v3; check.v1 appears in go.sum as yaml.v3'"'s test-module
checksum, not an implementation dependency. Trace files are JSONL under
impl/observe. Each output line includes the seed, outcome and trace path.
For a safety-only recheck of an existing trace:

"'```sh
impl/run.sh replay/scenarios/S1.yaml --check impl/observe/S1-seed-1.jsonl
```

This recheck does not grade scenario completion; normal scenario execution does.
The checker reconstructs state from serialized records, not member memory.
Generated checksums, traces and results derive from the clauses/cells named in
this README and their source/scenario derivation comments. go.sum is unannotated
machine-format dependency metadata derived from go.mod.
